AN  - 0435748c86ab9c614f773e13d9d93f3a955bb8ad
TI  - Plasma NET Markers Kinetics in COVID-19 Patients Receiving Immunomodulatory Treatments
PY  - 2023
AU  - Garcia, G
AU  - Dewitte, A
AU  - Labrouche-Colomer, S
AU  - Mouton, C
AU  - Delassasseigne, C
AU  - Menard, F
AU  - Vinclair, C
AU  - Begot, E
AU  - Petit, L
AU  - Carrie, C
AU  - et al.
AB  - Background: In COVID‐19, dexamethasone (DXM) and tocilizumab are used to alleviate inflammation in patients requiring oxygen support. Increased immunothrombosis, and NETosis in particular, is exacerbated in severe COVID‐19 patients. Whether the use of immunomodulatory drugs has any impact on NET kinetics is currently unknown. Whether mild patients may receive a benefit with the use of immunomodulatory drugs is being investigated in the COVERAGE trial (NCT04356495). Aims: We aimed to characterize the kinetics of plasma NET markers in COVID‐19 patients receiving immunomodulatory treatments. Methods: one hundred and sixty‐six early (≤7 days) COVID‐19 outpatients were randomly allocated to placebo group (79 i.e. 48%), inhaled ciclesonide (48 i.e. 29%) or inhaled IFN‐β‐1b (39 i.e. 23%). Fifty‐nine critically ill patients were included: 8 received placebo (14%), 42 (71%) DXM (6 mg i.v. for ten days) and nine (15%) DXM + tocilizumab (8 mg/kg I.v.). We quantified NET markers (MPO‐DNA, H3Cit, H3cit‐DNA) in plasma at inclusion, Day 3 and Day 7 for critically ill patients and at inclusion and Day 7 for outpatients. Results: Inhaled IFN‐β‐1b, but not inhaled ciclesonide nor placebo, was linked to a decrease in plasma H3Cit levels (p: 0.02). DXM alone was not linked to a decrease in plasma NET markers levels but the association of DXM + tocilizumab was associated with decreased MPO‐DNA levels (p: 0.03). Conclusion(s): Inhaled IFN‐β‐1b treatment is associated with a decrease in plasma H3Cit levels in early ambulatory mild COVID‐19 patients. DXM + tocilizumab, but not DXM alone, is associated with a decrease in plasma MPO‐DNA levels in critically ill COVID‐19 patients. NETosis appears to be a relevant adjunctive therapeutic target in COVID‐19 patients but better characterization of DNase activity defect in patients and animal models are urgently warranted.
UR  - https://www.epistemonikos.org/en/documents/0435748c86ab9c614f773e13d9d93f3a955bb8ad
T2  - Research and practice in thrombosis and haemostasis
VL  - 7
DO  - 10.1016/j.rpth.2023.100861
ER  - 


