AN  - 0630f1ae7146461837d80e2b70847795304ec2ef
TI  - Auxora Improves Outcomes in Patients With Severe COVID-19 Pneumonia: A Randomized Clinical Trial
PY  - 2021
AU  - Charles Bruen
AU  - Mukthar Al-Saadi
AU  - Edward Michelson
AU  - Maged Tanios
AU  - Raul Mendoza-Ayala
AU  - Joseph Miller
AU  - Jeffrey Zhang
AU  - Kenneth Stauderman
AU  - Sudarshan Hebbar
AU  - Peter Hou
AU  - CARDEA Study Investigators
AB  - BACKGROUND: Calcium release-activated calcium (CRAC) channel inhibitors block proinflammatory cytokine release, preserve endothelial integrity and may effectively treat patients with severe COVID-19 pneumonia. METHODS: CARDEA was a phase 2, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial evaluating the addition of Auxora, a CRAC channel inhibitor, to corticosteroids and standard of care (SOC) in adults with severe COVID-19 pneumonia. The primary endpoint was time to recovery through Day 60, with secondary endpoints of all-cause mortality at Day 60 and Day 30. Due to declining rates of COVID-19 hospitalizations and encroachment of prohibited medications as SOC, the study was stopped early. FINDINGS: In total, 141 patients were randomized to placebo and 143 to Auxora. The median time to recovery in patients receiving ≥ 1 dose of study drug (n=281) was seven vs eight days ( P =0.0420) for those receiving Auxora vs placebo, respectively. Time to recovery in patients with baseline imputed PaO 2 /FiO 2 ≤200 (n=261) was seven vs ten days ( P =0.0979), respectively. The all-cause mortality rate at Day 60 in patients with a baseline imputed PaO 2 /FiO 2 ≤200 was 13.8% vs 20.6% with Auxora vs placebo ( P =0.1449); Day 30 all-cause mortality was 7.7% and 17.6%, respectively ( P =0.0165). Similar trends were noted in pre-specified subgroups of patients on high flow nasal cannula at baseline and with a baseline imputed PaO 2 /FiO 2 ≤100. Serious adverse events occurred less frequently in patients treated with Auxora vs placebo. INTERPRETATION: Auxora was safe and well tolerated with strong signals in both time to recovery and all-cause mortality. CLINICAL TRIAL REGISTRATION DETAILS: NCT04345614.
