TI  - Effectiveness of Nirmatrelvir-Ritonavir, Molnupiravir, and Azvudine in Hospitalized Patients with COVID-19 Requiring Oxygen Supplementation: A Multicenter Retrospective Cohort Study
PY  - 2023
AU  - Yi Zhang
AU  - Xinrui Wang
AU  - Chunguo Jiang
AU  - Wenjiang Fu
AU  - Xuben Yu
AU  - Xiaojia Yu
AU  - Ciying Huang
AU  - Rui Zhao
AU  - Jun Hong
AU  - Yi Zhang
AU  - Jiachen Li
AU  - Rui Zhao
AU  - Zhuoling An
AU  - Zhaohui Tong
AB  - Background: Evidence on the efficacy of nirmatrelvir-ritonavir, molnupiravir, and azvudine efficacy in hospitalized patients requiring oxygen therapy is limited. Methods: We conducted a multicenter retrospective cohort study between November 15, 2022 to March 30, 2023 in five hospitals in China. Hospitalized COVID-19 patients who received oxygen therapy on admission were included. Patients who received nirmatrelvir-ritonavir, molnupiravir, and azvudine were propensity score-matched (PSM) with non-antiviral recipients. The primary outcome was 28-day all-cause mortality. Secondary outcomes included a composite outcome of 28-day disease progression, and each of these individual outcomes included invasive mechanical ventilation and intensive care unit admission. Findings: 4645 adult hospitalized COVID-19 patients were identified. After exclusion, 328 nirmatrelvir-ritonavir users, 133 molnupiravir users, and 136 azvudine users were included and matched with non-antiviral recipients. Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir (adjusted HR 0.46, 95% CI 0.32-0.68), molnupiravir (adjusted HR 0.53, 95% CI 0.31-0.90), and azvudine (adjusted HR 0.54, 95% CI 0.31-0.92) were associated with significantly lower risks of mortality compared to controls. The three antivirals were also associated with reduced risks of disease progression (nirmatrelvir-ritonavir: adjusted HR 0.48, 95% CI 0.34-0.68, molnupiravir: adjusted HR 0.47, 95% CI 0.29-0.76, azvudine: adjusted HR 0.56, 95% CI 0.34-0.94). No significant differences were found for invasive mechanical ventilation or ICU admission. Interpretation: Early initiation of nirmatrelvir-ritonavir, molnupiravir, or azvudine was associated with reduced mortality and disease progression in hospitalized COVID-19 patients requiring oxygen therapy. These findings support using oral antivirals for this patient population. Funding: This work was supported by grants from the Capital’s Funds for Health Improvement and Research (Grant No. 2022-1-1061) and the Ministry of Science and Technology of the People’s Republic of China (2021YFC0863600, 2023YFC0872500). Declaration of Interest: None to declare.  Ethical Approval: This study was approved by the institutional review board and ethics committee of Beijing Chaoyang Hospital, Capital Medical University (2023-683).
