AN  - 120c8010786e311806186add0c87a1a5d74ac15d
TI  - DOUBLE TROUBLE: A CASE OF CO-INFECTION WITH PSEUDOMONAS MENDOCINA AND GROUP G STREPTOCOCCUS CAUSING ARDS
PY  - 2021
AU  - Petrenko, D.
AU  - Vojtek, A.
AU  - Bishnoi, S.
AU  - Zirlinger, A.
AB  - TOPIC: Critical Care TYPE: Fellow Case Reports INTRODUCTION: Pseudomonas mendocina is an aerobic, Gram-negative rod that was first isolated from soil and water in 1970. Unlike Pseudomonas aeruginosa, whose pathogenicity is well-established, Pseudomonas mendocina was long thought to be a colonizer of urine and leg ulcers. This changed with the first account of Pseudomonas mendocina infectious endocarditis in 1992. To date, only 16 total cases of human disease have been published worldwide. Infective endocarditis and CNS infections represent half of these cases. Here we present the first case of polymicrobial sepsis with Pseudomonas mendocina manifesting as ARDS. CASE PRESENTATION: A 35 year old gentleman with an extensive past medical history of common variable immunodeficiency treated with weekly IVIG, heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, chronic deep venous thromboses, permanent atrial fibrillation on anticoagulation, chronic kidney disease at stage III, Klinefelter's syndrome, uncontrolled diabetes mellitus and morbid obesity presented from home to the emergency department with fever, epigastric pain, and shortness of breath. He was febrile, tachycardic, tachypneic, and hypoxic on arrival. On physical examination, the patient had an erythematous and edematous right anterior shin. His initial labs showed a mild leukocytosis, lactic acidosis, and acute kidney injury. Right foot x-ray showed mild edema, but no acute osteomyelitis. Blood cultures were drawn in the ED. He was admitted to the medical floor with acute hypoxic respiratory failure and sepsis. He developed worsening respiratory distress, was transferred to the ICU, and was eventually intubated. He was initially started on dexamethasone for suspected COVID-19, which eventually resulted as negative. The patient had persistent fevers and was then started on broad spectrum antibiotics with vancomycin and cefepime. He developed ARDS as a result of bacteremia, and acute renal failure requiring renal replacement therapy. Blood cultures grew group G streptococcus and Pseudomonas mendocina. Pseudomonas mendocina was pansensitive and suspected to be from his right lower extremity cellulitis. Antibiotics were narrowed and the patient was treated with a 14-day course of vancomycin and ceftriaxone. He was extubated on day 5 of hospitalization and completed his course of antibiotics. He recovered and was ultimately discharged. DISCUSSION: Little is known about Pseudomonas mendocina because it rarely causes disease in humans. As a result, no clear mode of transmission has been established. CONCLUSIONS: Most cases are successfully treated with third or fourth generation cephalosporins. There have been no reports of mortality and the clinical cure rate is 93.3%, suggesting that Pseudomonas mendocina has low virulence. REFERENCE #1: Aragone, M R et al. "Pseudomonas mendocina, an environmental bacterium isolated from a patient with human infective endocarditis." Journal of clinical microbiology vol. 30,6 (1992): 1583-4. doi:10.1128/JCM.30.6.1583-1584.1992 REFERENCE #2: Gani, Melony, et al. "Pseudomonas Mendocina Bacteremia: A Case Study and Review of Literature." American Journal of Case Reports, vol. 20, 2019, pp. 453–58. Crossref, doi:10.12659/ajcr.914360. REFERENCE #3: Ioannou, Petros, and Georgios Vougiouklakis. "A Systematic Review of Human Infections by Pseudomonas Mendocina." Tropical Medicine and Infectious Disease, vol. 5, no. 2, 2020, p. 71. Crossref, doi:10.3390/tropicalmed5020071. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Sonali Bishnoi, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Dimitry Petrenko, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Ashley Vojtek, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Andres Zirlinger, source=Web Response
