AB  - BACKGROUND: Remdesivir was studied in the Solidarity Finland study. METHODS: Patients hospitalized due to the COVID-19 disease were randomized to receive standard treatment or remdesivir in addition to it. In the Solidarity trial and the meta-analysis of randomized trials, the primary endpoint was in-hospital mortality. RESULTS: We recruited 208 patients from eleven hospitals. During hospitalization, 1% died in the remdesivir group (n = 114) and 4% in the standard treatment group (n = 94). 5% in both groups had to undergo invasive ventilator therapy. 11% in the remdesivir and 12% in the standard treatment group received intensive care. Liver enzymes were significantly elevated in 5% of the remdesivir group and 2% in the standard treatment group. In a subgroup analysis of the meta-analysis, remdesivir reduced the risk of death in patients who were not on ventilator therapy at the start of hospitalization (RR 0.85, 95% CI 0.75–0.96). CONCLUSIONS: In Finland, during the pandemic, it is possible to recruit a significant number of patients for large, randomized studies, where reliable results can be obtained quickly. Remdesivir can be helpful in hospitalized COVID-19 if started early.
