TI  - Maxillary Mucormycosis and Aspergillosis in post-COVID-19 patients in Mexico: A case series.
PY  - 2023
AU  - Urias-Barreras CM
AU  - Rodríguez-Archilla A
AU  - Canizalez-Roman A
AU  - Bastidas D
AU  - León-Sicairos NM
AB  - BACKGROUND: Fungal infections, during or as a consequence of SARS-CoV-2 infection, associated with uncontrolled diabetes mellitus and indiscriminate use of corticosteroids have been reported. In the jaw, mostly mucormycosis has been diagnosed in hospitals. METHODS: A retrospective, cross-sectional, descriptive study of the clinical, imaging, and histopathologic characteristics of maxillary invasive fungal infection in post-COVID-19 patients diagnosed in a private non-hospital oral pathology service in Mexico during 2020-2022 was conducted. RESULTS: We found 20 cases of mandibular invasive fungal infections in post-COVID-19 patients, 75% diagnosed as probable mucormycosis and 25% diagnosed as probable aspergillosis. The most common signs and symptoms were exposed necrotic bone followed by tooth mobility, discharge, and pain. On imaging, unilateral maxillary sinus involvement was observed in 6 cases (30%), and bilateral maxillary sinus involvement was observed in 3 cases (15%). CONCLUSIONS: It is essential to consider the association of osteonecrosis of the jaw in post-COVID-19 patients, with aspergillosis, not only mucormycosis, for early diagnosis and appropriate treatment.
