AN  - 491855f62f80c42d6a6fba1af8899e860d40c901
TI  - Glycemic Control in Post-COVID-19 Patients: A Retrospective Study
PY  - 2023
AU  - Golubykh, K.
AU  - Chen, Y.
AU  - Kovalenko, I.
AU  - Sharma Gautam, S.
AU  - Bobrysheva, H.
AU  - Calderon Martinez, E.
AU  - Atrash, A.
AU  - Wert, Y.
AB  - Introduction: Glucose dysregulation in patients with COVID-19 infection has been widely discussed. Yet, there is a lack of literature data supporting the worsening of preexistent Diabetes Mellitus (DM) and the development of new DM in patients with COVID-19. In this study, we aimed to explore the long-term changes in glucose control in patients after COVID-19 infection. Methods: In this retrospective study we included patients who were admitted with the diagnoses of COVID-19 and DM (type 1/type 2) in UPMC Central PA Healthcare System from 09/01/2020 to 09/01/2021. We used Student's t-test, Wilcoxon test and Kruskal-Walli's test to analyze between group differences for the continuous variables and Chi-square test for the categorical variables. Results: A total of 636 patients were included in the study with 309 (48.58%) males and 327 (51.42%) females. Mean patient age was 65 (SD= 13.5). The overall mean changes of A1C from COVID-19 infection to 3-6 months, 6-12 months were - 0.36 and -0.31, respectively. Subgroup analysis showed that patients treated for COVID-19 in the outpatient setting had a significant increase in hemoglobin A1C at both 3-6 months (p<0.0001) and 6-12 months (p=0.0003) as compared to hospitalized patients and those admitted to the intensive care unit (Figure 1). A total of 289 patients (45.4%) had a new hypoglycemic agent started, 148 patients (23.2%) had dose elevation, 24 patients (3.7%) experienced dose reduction, and 66 patients (10.4%) underwent agent discontinuation. Insulin (14.62%), SGLT2 (7.55%), and GLP-1(7.39%) were the most commonly added agents while metformin (3.30%), sulfonylurea (2.52%) and DPP-4 inhibitors (1.89%) were the most common agents removed. Discussion: Our study has shown that patients with COVID-19 had overall hemoglobin A1C level reduction 12 months after the infection which is inconsistent with current literature data. However, most of the patients who had hemoglobin A1c level reduction were hospitalized during COVID-19 infection. We suggest that patients who were monitored in the outpatient settings for COVID- 19 infection had less tight glycemic control with less modifications made to their antidiabetic regimen when compared to hospitalized population. The latter might indicate that COVID-19 disease severity might modify glycemic control through increasing providers' attention toward hospitalized population with DM. Moreover, we captured large proportion of patients experienced addition of the new hypoglycemic agents or had existing antidiabetic agents' dosages increased. These findings suggest the challenging nature of DM control post COVID-19 infection while explaining our findings of A1C reduction in overall patient population.
UR  - https://www.epistemonikos.org/en/documents/491855f62f80c42d6a6fba1af8899e860d40c901
T2  - American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine
VL  - 207
IS  - 1
SN  - 1535-4970
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm-conference.2023.A51
