AU  - COVID-19 Early Treatment Study Team Group
AB  - Background: Data from randomized controlled trials for the early treatment of mild-to-moderate COVID-19 during Omicron-era are sparse.  Methods: We included people with confirmed mild-to-moderate COVID-19 and at high-risk of progressing to severe disease (as by AIFA definition) seen for care in 5 outpatient clinical services for COVID-19 in Italy over January 2022-May 2023. The primary outcome was defined as the proportion of participants who experienced day-30 hospitalization due to severe COVID‐19 or all-cause death. Participants received either Nirmatrelvir/ritonavir (NMV/r), Molnupiravir (MLP), Remdesivir (RDV), Sotrovimab (SOT) or Tixagevimab/Cilgavimab (TIX/CIL) at recommended dosage and duration.  Results: We included 10,038 individuals: females 5,052 (50%), median age 71 years (IQR 59-81). 1,919 (19%) received SOT, 3,732 (37.2%) MLP, 1,444 (14%) RDV, 2,510 (25%) NMV/r and 433 (4%) TIX/CIL. Only 1,689 (17%) had none/incomplete vaccination and 2,435 (24.3%) with immunocompromised status. The overall rate of hospitalization/death was 2.40% (95% CI 2.10-2.71). Unadjusted rates according to interventions were 0.88% (95%CI 0.55-1.32) for NMV/r, 1.69% (95% CI 1.30-2.15) for MLP, 3.0% (95% CI 1.61-5.08) for TIX/CIL, 3.54% (95% CI 2.76-4.47) for SOT and 5.12% (95% CI 4.05-6.39) for RDV. Weighted analysis showed that NMV/r and MLP were superior to all other interventions. Data were inconclusive regarding the contrast NMV/r vs. MLP (weighted relative hazard (wRH)=0.84, 95% CI:0.49-1.43, p=0.52).  Conclusions:   In our population of mostly vaccinated individuals at high risk of progression to severe COVID-19 disease there was clinical benefit in using NMV/r or MLP instead of mAbs-based therapies or RDV. In absence of placebo-controlled randomized trials targeting fragile populations our data should help tailoring targeted AvD-based early therapies. Funding: The study was funded by the Italian Ministry of Health (Ricerca Corrente Linea 1) ACL work has been co-funded by the EuCARE project funded by the EU under the HORIZON Europe programme GA n. 101046016. Declaration of Interest: Valentina Siciliano declares Payment or honoraria for lectures, presentations, speakers bureaus, manuscript writing or educational events by Pfizer. Support for attending meetings by Astrazeneca. Simone Lanini declares: Consulting fees by GSK, ViiV, MSD and Gilead; Support for attending meetings by MSD, Gilead, ViiV. Silvia Meschi declares: Receipt of equipment, materials, drugs, medical writing, gifts or other services by Abbott srl Alessandra Vergori declares: Consulting fees by Gilead Sciences ViiV Healthcare; Support for attending meetings by MSD, Gilead, ViiV, Astrazeneca. Enrico Girardi declares: Grants or contracts from any entity (if not indicated in item #1 above) by Gilead (paid to INMI)and Mylan (paid to INMI); Payment or honoraria for lectures, presentations, speakers bureaus, manuscript writing or educational events by Gilead. Loredana Sarmati declares: Payment or honoraria for lectures, presentations, speakers bureaus, manuscript writing or educational events by Merck, Gilead, Abbvie, Angelini, Astra Zeneca, GSK; Support for attending meetings and/or travel by Gilead, Merck, Pfizer. Emanuele Nicastri declares: Consulting fees from Gliead Science; Payment or honoraria for lectures, presentations, speakers bureaus, manuscript writing or educational events by Roche; Support for attending meetings and/or travel by Pharmamar; Participation on a Data Safety Monitoring Board or Advisory Board by Takeda and Valneva Andrea Antinori declares: Grants or contracts from any entity (if not indicated in item #1 above) by Gilead (paid to INMI) Astra Zeneca (paid to INMI) ViiV Healthcare (paid to INMI); Consulting fees from Gilead, Astra Zeneca, Pfizer, Janssen-Cilag, Moderna, GSK, Merck, ViiV Healthcare; Payment or honoraria for lectures, presentations, speakers bureaus, manuscript writing or educational events by Gilead, Moderna, Pfizer, Merck, ViiV Healthcare; Support for attending meetings and/or travel by Gilead, Astra Zeneca. Remaining authors declare no conflicts of interest. Ethical Approval: The observational multicentric study protocol and the informed consent have been approved by the Ethical Committee of the National Institute for Infectious Diseases Lazzaro Spallanzani (approval number: n. 380, 30/09/2021. FAV del Registro delle Sperimentazioni 2020/2021).
