TY  - JOUR
AN  - 6563082bd7ac8bcb23fb8c1ff597b7b7daef23e1
TI  - Association and Additive Risk of Respiratory and Viral Response Baseline Variables With Mortality in Therapeutics for Inpatients With COVID-19 (TICO) Platform Randomized Clinical Trial (RCT) Participants
PY  - 2023
AU  - Aggarwal, NR
AU  - Nordwall, J
AU  - Der, T
AU  - Eriobu, N
AU  - Ginde, AA
AU  - Hayanga, JW
AU  - Highbarger, HC
AU  - Lundgren, J
AU  - Laverdure, S
AU  - Pett, S
AU  - et al.
AB  - Rationale: Mortality in adults hospitalized with COVID‐19 is unacceptably high, emphasizing the need to better understand the association of baseline respiratory and viral response variables with mortality to risk stratify and prioritize therapies. Methods: We assessed baseline data among 2625 adults hospitalized for acute SARS‐CoV‐2 infection and enrolled in the TICO platform trial (NCT04501978) from August 2020 ‐ November 2021 across 112 sites on four continents. Plasma SARS‐CoV‐2 nucleocapsid (viral) antigen level, anti‐Spike RBD (spike) and anti‐N antibodies (Ab) were measured centrally. We evaluated baseline variables for association with Day 90 mortality using univariate and multivariate Cox proportional hazards models. We adjusted multivariable models for age, gender, race/ethnicity, residence, geographical region, infection period, active or placebo treatment group, and baseline pulmonary status and reported adjusted hazard ratio (aHR, 95% CI), significance (P<0.05); aHR > 1 signified higher mortality risk. Results: Median age was 57 years (46, 68), 58% were male, 42% Black or Hispanic, and 21% vaccinated. 9.9% had COPD, 6.4% had asthma, and 1.9% required premorbid oxygen therapy. Pre‐randomization COVID‐19 treatments included: corticosteroids (68%), remdesivir (61.3%), immune modulators (6.3%). Viral antigen level (ng/L) was divided into quartiles: <200:23.9%, 200‐1499:26.8%, 1500‐4499:23.3%, ≥4500:25.9%; 51.6% and 62.4% were positive for anti‐spike or anti‐N Ab. We quantified breathing difficulty by Borg dyspnea scale in 92%, with 47.8% reporting “none to slight”, 32.5% “moderate to somewhat severe”, and 19.7% “severe to maximal”. Pre‐randomization oxygen requirements were: no oxygen:25.1%, O2 < 4L:36.2%, O2 ≥ 4L:27.8%, and NIV/HFNO:10.9%. In multivariable analyses, independent variables associated with 90 day mortality included: remdesivir use (0.65, 0.48‐0.86), viral antigen ≥4500 (2.99, 2.02‐4.43 compared to <200), +anti‐spike Ab (0.59, 0.46‐0.77), +anti‐N Ab (0.74, 0.57‐0.97), Borg scale severe to maximal (1.47, 1.04‐2.05 compared to none to slight), O2 < 4L (1.74, 1.05‐2.89), O2 ≥ 4L (5.15, 3.26‐8.14), and NIV/HFNO (15.33, 9.27‐25.38). Further adjusting for viral Ag level eliminated significance for +anti‐spike or anti‐N Abs; yet viral Ag ≥4500 remained significant (3.14, 2.03‐4.84) after adjusting for Ab status. We further examined the relationship of viral antigen levels with these variables, demonstrating additive impact on hazard for mortality with moderate‐high oxygen requirements, most notably among participants with viral antigen level ≥4500 and NIV/HFNO (aHR 40.9, 13.97‐116.1) (Table 1). Conclusions: A predominantly unvaccinated cohort comprised of TICO RCT participants demonstrated strong associations of respiratory‐ and viral response‐focused baseline variables with mortality, with major additive risk when combining higher levels of respiratory support and viral antigen.
