AN  - 75389ee73d86a914303e7ea1b00380e8ce233265
TI  - Change in Effectiveness of Sotrovimab for Preventing Hospitalization and Mortality in COVID-19 Outpatients During the Omicron Phase
PY  - 2022
AU  - Neil  R. Aggarwal
AU  - Laurel  E. Beaty
AU  - Tellen  D. Bennett
AU  - Nichole  E. Carlson
AU  - David  A. Mayer
AU  - Kyle  C. Molina
AU  - Jennifer Peers
AU  - Seth Russell
AU  - Matthew  K. Wynia
AU  - Adit  A. Ginde
AB  - Background:  Sotrovimab, a neutralizing monoclonal antibody (mAb) treatment authorized for early symptomatic COVID-19 patients, effectively prevented progression to severe disease and mortality following infection with pre-Omicron SARS-CoV-2 variants. We sought to determine whether sotrovimab is similarly effective against SARS-CoV-2 Omicron variant infection.   Methods:  Observational cohort study of non-hospitalized adult patients with SARS-CoV-2 infection from December 26, 2021 to March 10, 2022 (>96% Omicron BA.1 variant), using electronic health records from a statewide health system linked to vaccine and mortality data. We propensity matched up to 3 patients not receiving authorized treatment for each patient treated with sotrovimab  .  The primary outcome was 28-day hospitalization; secondary outcomes included mortality. Separately, we propensity matched sotrovimab-treated patients from the Omicron or Delta phases to each other and then to variant matched untreated controls. Logistic regression was used to determine sotrovimab effectiveness during Omicron and for differences between variant phases. Findings:  Of 30,247 SARS-CoV-2 Omicron variant infected outpatients, we matched 1,542 receiving sotrovimab to 3,663 not receiving treatment. Sotrovimab treatment was not associated with reduced odds of 28-day hospitalization (2.5% versus 3.2%; adjusted OR 0.82, 95% CI 0.55, 1.19) or mortality (0.1% versus 0.2%; adjusted OR 0.62, 95% CI 0.07, 2.78). Between phases, the observed treatment odds ratio was higher during Omicron than during Delta (OR 0.85 vs. 0.39, respectively; interaction  p=0.053). Interpretation:  Real-world evidence demonstrated sotrovimab was not associated with reduced 28-day hospitalization or mortality among COVID-19 outpatients during the Omicron BA.1 phase. Funding: U.S. National Institutes of Health Declaration of Interest: None to declare.  Ethical Approval: The study was approved by the Colorado Multiple Institutional Review Board with a waiver of informed consent
