TI  - Chlorpheniramine Intranasal Spray (0.4[percnt]) to Accelerate COVID-19 Clinical Recovery in an Outpatient Setting: accros III
PY  - 2023
AU  - Valerio Pascua, FR
AU  - Pineda, E
AU  - Vargas-Pineda, M
AU  - Caceres-Gutierrez, L
AU  - Garay-Umanzor, J
AU  - Pineda-Castellanos, A
AU  - Bulnes-Aguirre, M
AU  - Tesh, M
AU  - Cabrera-Alonso, A
AU  - Chauhan, Z
AU  - et al.
AB  - Rationale Previously, our group demonstrated the safety, efficacy, and antiviral effect of intranasally administered Chlorpheniramine Maleate (CPM) for treating coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID‐19). Since the nasal cavity is the portal of entry for COVID pathogens, upper respiratory symptoms (URS) such as cough and nasal congestion are significant symptoms in the course of the disease. Intranasal therapies could alleviate the disease‐induced URS faster. This study evaluated the effectiveness and safety of intranasal CPM for treating mild to moderate COVID‐19‐ induced URS in the outpatient setting. MethodsThe Accelerating COVID‐19 Clinical Recovery in an Outpatient Setting (ACCROS) study was conducted using a randomized, double‐blinded, placebo‐controlled trial (ACCROS‐III). Patients with mild to moderate COVID‐19 confirmed by reverse transcription‐polymerase chain reaction were enrolled. The primary endpoint in was time to clinical recovery, defined as the change from baseline to day 7 in COVID‐19 symptoms, defined as the percent change (Δ%) in daily symptoms score (DSS) and the severity of the disease symptoms using a visual analog scale (VAS), on a scale of 1‐10 (10=worst symptoms). COVID‐19 patients (n = 136) were recruited and assigned to either a 10‐day CPM treatment (n=71) or placebo (PLB) (n=65) in addition to standard of care (SoC). Secondary endpoints included the incidence of hospitalization and the proportion of patients with URS of cough and nasal congestion on day 7. Student's T‐tests and Chi‐square (X2) analyses were conducted to detect the statistical significance (P<0.05) between the groups for continuous and categorical variables, respectively. Results There was a difference in the rate of clinical recovery in Δ% DSS (M ‐11.7 ± SEM 8.6%; P=0.089) and Δ%VAS (‐8.2 ± 4.0%; P=0.026), such that the CPM group reported fewer symptoms than PLB (Figure 1A). The proportion of patients who reported URS at day 7 was significantly lower (X2=4.92; P=0.027) in CPM vs. PLB for nasal congestion (14.5% vs. 32.7%) but not for cough (36.4% vs. 48.1%; X2=1.51; P=0.220) (Figure 1B). None of the patients required hospitalization. Conclusions The result of this two‐part study supports the conclusion that intranasal CPM is an antiviral agent that can be administered in tranasally to treat COVID‐19‐induced symptoms effectively. Intranasal CPM accelerates clinical recovery and reduces URS in patients with mild to moderate COVID‐ 19. This study's important implications include individuals returning to daily life faster, reducing community and individual economic burdens, and decreasing healthcare utilization.
