TY  - JOUR
AN  - 8482d078ca08c83f2bdf3bcb992fc70b3365719c
TI  - A cross-sectional research conducted to assess pharmacists' knowledge, attitude, practice, and readiness in the presence of the COVID-19 epidemic in Baghdad.
PY  - 2022
AU  - Al-Obaidi M
AU  - Mahmood A
AU  - Al-Adhamy M
AB  - Background   - Usually, community pharmacies that are expected to take extraordinary efforts to prevent client cross-infection sell masks and disinfectants. Additionally, it is their duty to take the lead in informing the public about this outbreak. Therefore, the study aims to test Iraqi pharmacists' knowledge of COVID-19. Methods   - Numerous pharmacies in Baghdad were randomly visited outside of lockdown hours and had to complete a Google form following each visit. The form had simple checkboxes for all desired observations and specialized spaces for any additional information that needed to be noted. The survey's statistical analyses were descriptive, and no formal hypothesis testing was performed. Numbers and percentages were used to summarize the results. Results   - Iraqi pharmacists trusted the WHO, media and websites, and Ministry of health (MOH) websites for COVID-19 information. According to the data, pharmacists administered acetaminophen and corticosteroids for COVID-19 infection treatment. The results also showed that Zing, Vitamin C, and Vitamin D were recommended to improve immunity and that these medicines were given orally. Massive numbers of pharmacists suggest that the call center of MOH is not applicable. Conclusion   - the Iraqi Ministry of Health did not include pharmacists in its health emergency preparations. This resulted in a general delay in pharmacies' readiness. National governments and organizations may benefit from using pharmacists' knowledge and skills to reduce or stop future COVID-19 waves and manage risk if these waves do happen.
UR  - https://www.epistemonikos.org/en/documents/8482d078ca08c83f2bdf3bcb992fc70b3365719c
T2  - ResearchSquare
DO  - 10.21203/rs.3.rs-1863456/v1
ER  - 


TY  - JOUR
AN  - 84846929aa64136523f02d79f366aeadfcb53976
TI  - Role of Potential Biomarkers in Management and Prognosis of Corona Virus Disease.
PY  - 2022
AU  - Khatri A
AB  - Corona virus disease is a global pandemic. One of the key issues has been the very high volume of patients presenting to health centres or hospitals during the outbreak. It clearly overwhelms the human and mechanistic capacities available. Therefore, early and effective predictors of clinical outcomes are required for risk stratification. MATERIAL: This is hospital based retrospective study on patients who were admitted in Covid wards/ICU at IGGMC. Patients were divided into 3 groups- mild-moderate; severe; and critical based on their clinical presentation on admission. Several biomarkers like WBC, platelets, N/L, CRP, LDH, S. ferritin, d-dimer, CPK-MB, Serum creatinine, BUL, SGOT, SGPT, Serum albumin were analysed before and after treatment. OBSERVATION: 110 patients were enrolled in this study, 36 were classified into mild-moderate, 56 into severe and 18 into critical. As all of mild-moderate patients were discharged and majority of critical patients expired, biomarkers were compared between severe patients who were discharged vs severe patients who died. Out of these biomarkers, CRP was significantly decreased during course of treatment in severe patients who were discharged (p = 0.004) in comparison to severe patients who died where CRP was significantly increased (p = 0.001) (p value of difference being 0.00001). There was also significant change in ferritin levels (p = 0.006), while other biomarkers like WBC (p = 0.07), platelets (p = 0.066), N/L (p = 0.3), LDH (p = 0.06), d-dimer (p = 0.1), CPK-MB (p = 0.49), serum creatinine (p = 0.05), urea (p = 0.06), S. albumin (p= 0.3), SGOT (p =0.07), SGPT (p=0.25) did not show promising results. In addition, various treatment protocols were analysed by comparing CRP before and after treatment. Severe patients were divided into 2 groups, who took injection Remdesivir along with antibiotics, LMWH, systemic steroids vs who didn't, and CRP level were compared, but the difference was not significant (p = 0.06). Pre and post treatment CRP was also compared for Tocilizumab, Fevipiravir, Hydroxychloroquine, Doxycyline, but none of them were able to decrease CRP significantly (p > 0.05) in the severe or critical group but these drugs were effective in reducing CRP significantly (p<0.05) when given in mild-moderate group or if the treatment was started early. CONCLUSION: Increment in CRP and ferritin could effectively predict clinical outcome and could be used for risk stratification but no available drug is effective in reducing these biomarkers significantly in severe or critical group.
