AU  - Sittiruk Roytrakul
AU  - Amonpun Rattanasrisomporn
AU  - Natthasit Tansakul
AU  - Jatuporn Rattanasrisomporn
AB  - Feline infectious peritonitis (FIP) and COVID-19 are both caused by coronaviruses, but they are different viruses that affect different species. This study aimed to investigate the clinical and laboratory characteristics of naturally occurring FIP in order to diagnose FIP more efficiently and rapidly, and to estimate the median survival time of FIP cats treated with prednisolone to guide further therapeutic planning. In this retrospective study, data from a total of 116 cats with effusion who visited Kasetsart University Veterinary Teaching Hospital between December 2016 and October 2022 were fully recorded and analyzed. Forty-five FIP-diagnosed cats were enrolled to analyze the clinical and laboratory findings and to calculate the median lifespan from the date of diagnosis to the final day. The study findings indicate that FIP was a common disease affecting cats aged 1–2 years, and was highly prevalent among male cats. Clinical manifestations of FIP affected the digestive (60%), hematological (53.3%), respiratory (33.3 %), neurological (6.7%), and ocular (4.4%) systems. Blood profiles revealed mild anemia, lymphopenia, thrombocytopenia, hypoalbuminemia, hyperglobulinemia, and A:G ratio at 0.4. In cats suspected to have FIP, fluid analysis and cytology demonstrated a transparent yellow fluid with protein content of 6 mg% and a total nucleated cell count of approximately 5,000–10,000 cells. During the observation period, FIP cats treated with prednisolone exhibited a median survival time of 31 days. In conclusion, confirming FIP in antemortem cases can be challenging, therefore tentative diagnosis of FIP must be made with care. This study provided practical diagnostic tools to diagnose FIP based on the cats’ clinical signs or multiple abnormalities which allow more efficient and rapid detection.
