AU  - Ahmet Gül
AB  - Background: Hyperinflammation with features of cytokine storm developing in 2nd week of COVID-19 contributes to the worse outcome. Because of relatively milder inflammatory findings compared to other cytokine storms, available criteria for classification of hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis or macrophage activation syndrome (MAS) could not be helpful. Methods: Discovery cohort included symptomatic COVID-19 patients, who were followed at hospital during the initial wave. Replication cohort consisted of hospitalized patients from a later period, and all required oxygen support and received glucocorticoids. Diagnosis of MAS was made by an expert panel and the majority received tocilizumab or anakinra. Daily clinical and laboratory data were recorded, and data of treatment start day were compared with the 5-6th day data of non-MAS group. Receiver operating characteristics (ROC) and values maximising the sensitivity and specificity of each parameter were calculated to determine criteria items. Findings: 685 patients were analysed in the discovery and 156 in the replication cohorts; of whom 150 and 61 received the treatment for MAS, respectively. Mortality rate was higher in the patients with MAS in the discovery cohort (23·3%) compared to the rate of non-MAS (3·7%) patients; and it was much lower in the replication cohort for both groups. The new 12-item criteria were developed to define COVID-19-associated pulmonary MAS (pMAS) early, and the score of 35 provided 85·3% sensitivity and 81.7% specificity. The same criteria gave 90·0% sensitivity for the diagnosis of pMAS in the replication cohort, but much lower specificity scores were observed, due to the control group including milder cases of MAS. Interpretation: The new set of criteria is expected to help define patients with COVID-19 associated pMAS with a reasonable sensitivity and specificity and enable us to start treatments as early as possible.  Funding Information: This study was partially funded by The Scientific and Technological Research Council of Turkey COVID-19 Platform. Declaration of Interests: No industry affiliations regarding this study are declared. Ethics Approval Statement: The study protocol was approved by the Ethics Committee of Istanbul Faculty of Medicine and Ministry of Healthy COVID-19 registry.
