TY  - JOUR
AN  - b39420d4b5b656ff46de6dea6fc08ea82bdd0182
TI  - Clinical trial to determine safety and efficacy of intravenous silver nanoparticles in severe covid patients
PY  - 2022
AU  - Sarkar S. 
AU  - College of Medicine and Sagore Dutta Hospital 
AB  - Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Corona Virus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) causing coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) has devastated our mankind. Till now, no approved treatment is available to combat this disease. At present prophylactic vaccination is the only effective way to prevent the progression to severe disease. Oxygen therapy, corticosteroids and anti-thrombotic medicines are the only drugs proven to be effective, but they do not prevent death in severe cases. Silver nanoparticle (AgNP) is a well-established antimicrobial agent effective against many bacteria and fungi. Antiviral activity of AgNP is also been investigated against different types of viruses, including HIV, influenza virus, Hepatitis B virus, Monkey Pox virus, Herpes simplex virus, Tacaribe virus, and other respiratory viruses. Although many studies have highlighted the potential of AgNPs against SARS-CoV-2, this study is the first to prove this with a clinical trial. This open-ended, randomized, parallel group, active controlled, investigator initiated clinical trial (IIS) was performed with 40 moderate to severely ill COVID-19 patients in India.   The IIS was performed in India in June 2021, during Delta peak and January 2022, during Omicron peak. The study included 40 patients with moderately severe or severe COVID-19 pneumonia, fulfilling the inclusion criteria: 20 patients were included in the case group and 20 patients in the control group. Each treatment group consists of 10 patients diagnosed with Delta variant and 10 patients with Omicron variant of SARS-CoV-2. AgNPs were supplied by BHS Medical Solutions GmbH in the form of the product AgSept® used for research purpose. AgNPs were 99.99% pure, with a size distribution of 10 nm, spherical shape and good water solubility. The human equivalent dose of 0.3 mg/kg was calculated based on previous literature. To get as less adverse effects as possible, 30% of the total calculated NOAEL dose for human (i.e., 5.4 mg of water soluble AgNP) were divided in three (3) doses, administered in three consecutive days as 10% each day. There was a trend of more favorable outcome (discharged after clinically becoming stale) in the adjunctive AgNP group compared to treatment as usual comparator group. Unfavorable outcome including death were more in the control group. From this study we could not find any statistically significant difference in base line variables including age, sex, comorbidities and EWS severity between different outcome group, favorable vs. unfavorable. Additionally, significant differences were observed in oxygen supply needed in case versus control group indicating positive effects of AgNPs in treatment of pneumonia. As this was the first study of its kind, further research is needed to evaluate the impact of AgNPs in COVID-19 treatment.
UR  - https://www.epistemonikos.org/en/documents/b39420d4b5b656ff46de6dea6fc08ea82bdd0182
T2  - CTRI
ER  - 


TY  - JOUR
AN  - b395a7228bdbbc6e72144986ba7d2f74930aa797
TI  - Impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on prescription refills for immune-mediated inflammatory disorders: a time series analysis (January 2019 to January 2021) using the English Prescribing Dataset.
PY  - 2022
AU  - Barrett R
AU  - Barrett R
AU  - Lin SX
AU  - Culliford D
AU  - Fraser S
AU  - Edwards CJ
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To investigate monthly prescription refills for common immunosuppressive/immunomodulatory therapy (sulfasalazine, hydroxychloroquine, azathioprine, methotrexate, leflunomide) prescriptions in England during the complete first wave of the COVID-19 pandemic. Secondary analysis examined unit cost analysis and regional use. DESIGN AND SETTING: A national cohort of community-based, primary care patients who anonymously contribute data to the English Prescribing Dataset, dispensed in the community in England, were included. Descriptive statistics and interrupted time series analysis over 25 months (14 months before, 11 months after first lockdown) were evaluated (January 2019 to January 2021, with March 2020 as the cut-off point). OUTCOME MEASURES: Prescription reimbursement variance in period before the pandemic as compared with after the first lockdown. RESULTS: Fluctuation in monthly medicines use is noted in March 2020: a jump is observed for hydroxychloroquine (Mann-Whitney, SE 14.652, standardised test statistic 1.911, p value=0.059) over the study period. After the first lockdown, medicines use fluctuated, with wide confidence intervals. Unit-cost prices changed substantially: sulfasalazine 33% increase, hydroxychloroquine 98% increase, azathioprine 41% increase, methotrexate 41% increase, leflunomide 20% decrease. London showed the least quantity variance, suggesting more homogeneous prescribing and patient access compared with Midlands and East of England, suggesting that some patients may have received medication over/under requirement, representing potential resource misallocation and a proxy for adherence rates. Changepoint detection revealed four out of the five medicines' use patterns changed with a strong signal only for sulfasalazine in March/April 2020. CONCLUSIONS: Findings potentially present lower rates of adherence because of the pandemic, suggesting barriers to care access. Unit price increases are likely to have severe budget impacts in the UK and potentially globally. Timely prescription refills for patients taking immunosuppressive/immunomodulatory therapies are recommended. Healthcare professionals should identify patients on these medicines and assess their prescription-day coverage, with planned actions to flag and follow-up adherence concerns in patients.
UR  - https://www.epistemonikos.org/en/documents/b395a7228bdbbc6e72144986ba7d2f74930aa797
T2  - BMJ open
VL  - 12
SP  - e051936
IS  - 12
SN  - 2044-6055
DO  - 10.1136/bmjopen-2021-051936
ER  - 


