TI  - Emergence and fixation of SARS-CoV-2 minority variants in a chronically infected patient receiving therapy in Denmark
PY  - 2024
AU  - Fonager J
AU  - Nytofte NJS
AU  - Schouw CH
AU  - Poulsen CB
AU  - Wiese L
AU  - Fomsgaard A
AU  - Bennedbæk M
AU  - Rasmussen M
AU  - Nielsen XC
AB  - SARS-CoV-2 variants of concern (VOC), such as Delta and Omicron have harbored mutations, which increased viral infectivity or ability to evade neutralizing antibodies. Immunocompromised patients might be a source of some of these emerging variants. In this study, we sequenced 17 consecutive samples from an immunocompromised patient with a long-term SARS-CoV-2 infection with the pre-VOC era lineage B.1.177.35. We here describe the emergence of 73 nonsynonymous minority variants in this patient and show that 10 of these mutations became dominant in the viral population during the treatment period. Four of these were seen throughout the infection period and had a very low global prevalence, although three of them were also observed later in the Alpha, Delta, and Omicron lineages. We also found that two adjacent nsp12 variants (M785I and S786P) belonged to different quasi-species and competed during the early stages of infection and remdesivir administration. This emphasizes the importance of ongoing genome surveillance of SARS-CoV-2 among immunocpromised patients.
UR  - https://www.epistemonikos.org/en/documents/b7acc718047137db9ad88a352259a1ebe1da2d15
