TY  - JOUR
AN  - bde56f75f250ed2e584529d16b5bcc7239b30e85
TI  - Therapeutic Effects of Clarithromycin Combined with Molnupiravir in Management of Mild to Moderate COVID-19 Hospitalized Patients: A Case Control Study
PY  - 2022
AU  - Kenji Gonda
AU  - Kouichi Suzuki
AU  - Koji Kono
AU  - Seiichi Takenoshita
AB  - Background: Molnupiravir and clarithromycin (CAM) are used to treat Coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19). However, no evidence is available regarding the safety and efficacy of these combination therapy. Methods: Of the 109 patients, we conducted a case control study of 28 uninfected rehabilitative patients and 91 COVID-19 infected rehabilitative patients in the same ward and room. All infected patients received oral molnupiravir (800 mg per day; 400 mg twice daily for 5 days). Among them, patients with respiratory symptoms were administered CAM in combination (400 mg per day; 200 mg twice daily for 3 days), and asymptomatic patients were administered molnupiravir alone. Blood biomarkers were evaluated at the time of hospitalization before infection, and on day 5 and 14 after oral administration. Findings: Fifty-four patients took molnupiravir plus CAM and 37 patients took molnupiravir alone. In the CAM combined administration group, the blood biomarker values of day 5 were lower than those of molnupiravir alone administration group, and the values on the 14th day were approximately the same as the values of the uninfected patient. Uninfected patients had higher IgA levels and lower sIL2R levels than infected patients. In the CAM combined administration group, IgA increased and sIL2R decreased on the 14  th  day than on the 5  th  day. In addition, the CAM combined administration group on day 14 had higher IgA levels and lower sIL2R levels than the molnupiravir single administration group. Independent risk predictors were D-dimer (OR 1.08, 95% CI 1.05 to 1.11, p <0.05), IgA (OR 1.06, 95% CI 1.02 to 1.10, p <0.05), and sIL2R (OR 1.13, 95% CI 1.09 to 1.17, P <0.05). Furthermore, compared with the molnupiravir alone administration group, the CAM combination administration group had lower IgA levels and higher sIL2R levels, but significantly prolonged the mean survival time (P = 0.01, P = 0.004). Interpretation: In COVID-19 patients with mild to moderate respiratory symptoms, concomitant administration of molnupiravir plus CAM did not increase blood biomarkers, elevated immune activity, and did not aggravate disease. Early molnupiravir plus CAM administration may be beneficial for early control of symptomatic patients and also for asymptomatic early PCR positive patients. Trial Registration Details: MOVe-OUT ClinicalTrials.gov number, NCT04575597). Funding Information: Funded by Merck Sharp and Dohme. Declaration of Interests: All funds have not been paid to any institute, and all of authors has not received any personal payments. All authors declare no competing interests.  Ethics Approval Statement: Approved by the Ethics Committee of Daido Central Hospital (Approval ID:daido0037).
