AU  - Bardou-Jacquet, E
AU  - et al.
AB  - Introduction: Nirmatrelvir/ritonavir showed an 89% risk‐reduction of hospitalization for patients at high risk for severe COVID‐19 in a randomized controlled trial. The drug appears, therefore, particularly adapted to immunosuppressed patients including solid organ transplant recipients, who altogether hardly develop immunity towards SARS‐CoV‐2 after vaccination and are at high‐risk for severe COVID‐19. We report the experience of outpatient nirmatrelvir/ritonavir treatment in liver transplant recipients (LTRs) in real‐life practice. Material and methods: Non‐severe COVID‐19 LTRs treated with nirmatrelvir/ritonavir were included. All concomitant treatments, clinical and biological data were collected and a complete review of DDIs was performed by pharmacologists. Therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM) was required at the end of the treatment. This monitoring was repeated at 3 weeks after the end of treatment. A phone call with the hepatologist was conducted to collect adverse drug reactions, adverse events and the patient's clinical status. Results: Twenty‐seven LTRs were included in the study. The percentage of liver transplant recipients who received with ≥3 COVID vaccine doses was 92.6%. At baseline patients were treated with tacrolimus (81.5%), cyclosporine (7.4%), mycophenolic acid (77.8%), everolimus (11.1%) and/or corticosteroids (14.8%). None of the patients have been hospitalized. Seventeen patients (63.0%) presented an adverse drug reaction (2 discontinuations) (taste disturbance [51.9%] and digestive disorders [29.6%]). Twenty‐three patients benefited from TDM and only one patient presented with an elevated tacrolimus blood concentration (42.8 ng/mL) without any clinical consequences. Discussion/Conclusion: We have shown that it is possible to treat LTRs in an outpatient setting with a multidisciplinary management of COVID‐19 treatment in a reference center. A joint pharmacologist and clinician‐based approach of the nirmatrelvir/ritonavir prescription secures LTRs therapy by ensuring an expert‐ although remote‐ management. TDM remained the key player for calcineurin inhibitors monitoring in particular and the centralization of assay and expert advice aiming at piloting dosage adjustments and treatment restart process. [1].
