TY  - JOUR
AN  - caf1e0063d9bd2b6b80ce0501bad8b229d71db7a
TI  - Pulmonary Function Before and After COVID-19 in a Mid-South US Cohort
PY  - 2022
AU  - Edmonds, P.J.
AU  - Watchmaker, J.
AU  - Kerchberger, V.E.
AU  - Sevin, C.M.
AB  - Rationale: Over 45 million people in the US have survived COVID-19. The severity of illness ranges from self-limited symptoms to prolonged hospitalization requiring intensive care. Multiple reports show that COVID-19 survivors have impaired pulmonary function. However, little is known about change from baseline function. We assessed changes in pulmonary function before and after COVID-19 among patients receiving care at our center. Methods: Retrospective cohort study of PCR-confirmed COVID-19 patients between March 2020 and June 2021 with both pre-COVID pulmonary function tests (PFTs) and post-COVID PFTs. Demographics and comorbidities (obesity, tobacco use, heart disease, malignancy, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, interstitial lung disease, and hematopoietic or solid organ transplant) were collected. Severity of illness was identified using the World Health Organization scale (mild/moderate [clinical symptoms or infiltrate on chest radiograph with SpO2>90%] severe/critical [SpO2<90%, respiratory rate >30 or ARDS, shock, multiorgan failure]). We assessed differences in pre- and post-COVID PFTs using paired ttests, and univariate logistic regression to test associations between clinical variables and change in PFTs. Results: Eight-eight patients were identified. Median age was 56.3 (IQR 44-66), 57 (65%) were female, and median BMI was 29.5 (IQR 25-35). Common comorbidities included hypertension (49%), asthma (44%), immunosuppression (38%), ILD (35%), and transplant (23%). Sixty-seven (76%) had mild/moderate disease and 21 (24%) were severe/critical. Inpatient care was required for 34 (39%). Median time from pre-COVID PFTs to COVID-19 diagnosis was 208 days (IQR 91- 580). Median time to follow-up PFTs was 120 days (IQR 69-168). COVID-19 was not associated with change in FVC, FEV1, FEV1/FVC ratio, RV, or TLC, but was associated with a significant decrease in DLCO (mean: -9.5%; 95%CI: -14 to -4.8; p=0.0002). Survivors of severe/critical COVID-19 had more substantial DLCO decrements (mean: -19%; 95%CI: -30 to -8.0]; p=0.0023) than did survivors of mild/moderate disease (mean: -5.0%; 95%CI -9.1 to -0.9; p=0.018). Change in DLCO was associated with severity of illness (OR 0.92 [CI 0.86-0.98], p=0.015), hospitalization (OR 0.93 [CI 0.86-0.98], p=0.019), new oxygen requirement (OR 0.93 [CI 0.85-0.97), p=0.0112), treatment with remdesivir (OR 0.94 [CI 0.89 to 0.99], p=0.036, and dexamethasone (OR 0.91 [CI 0.83 to 0.97], p=0.0098). Conclusion: COVID-19 survivors had decreased diffusion capacity on PFTs following recovery from infection. Severity of illness, hospitalization and new oxygen requirement at discharge were also associated with change in DLCO. These data can inform clinical monitoring of this vulnerable population and will merit further follow-up studies of the longterm pulmonary consequence of COVID-19.
UR  - https://www.epistemonikos.org/en/documents/caf1e0063d9bd2b6b80ce0501bad8b229d71db7a
T2  - American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine
VL  - 205
