AU  - Soliven S
AU  - Sintef A
AU  - Palmer-Toy D
AU  - Platz B
AU  - Shafi H
AU  - Zemek A
AB  - BACKGROUND: In April 2020, the Mayo Clinic helped establish the US FDA Expanded Access Protocol (EAP) for COVID19 Convalescent Plasma (CCP). The effectiveness of CCP in the published literature is contradictory, as some retrospective studies showed benefit in reducing mortality and severe illness, while prospective randomized controlled trials demonstrated no benefit of CCP. OBJECTIVES: In this brief, we discuss 1) the implementation of CCP across Kaiser Permanente Southern California between April 2020 and April 2021, 2) retrospective multivariable analysis of 2,831 COVID19 patients who were transfused with CCP compared to 18,475 COVID19 patients who did not receive CCP, 3) how to reconcile contradictory published data regarding the efficacy of CCP, and 4) guidance regarding the future use of convalescent plasma in a large community hospital setting. METHODS: Multivariate analysis was controlled for demographic characteristics, level of oxygen delivery, ICU care, selected laboratory findings, and other concurrent treatment-related variables. Tubing segments from 151 CCP units transfused between October 2020 and April 2021 were retrospectively tested for SARS-COV-2 anti-spike protein RBD IgG. OUTCOMES: Multivariable analysis showed CCP transfusion did not affect mortality rates at 30-days and 5-months (OR 1.04, 95%CI [0.87 to 1.25] and HR 1.05, 95%CI [0.93 to 1.19]). CONCLUSIONS: If convalescent plasma is offered as a therapeutic in a future viral pandemic, we recommend: 1) only transfusing patients who are negative for neutralizing antibodies, 2) transfusing very early during the disease course, 3) only using convalescent plasma with known levels of neutralizing antibodies, or 4) consider alternatively providing fractionated hyperimmune globulin.
