TI  - Latent Class Analysis Identifies Novel Phenotypes of Severe COVID-19 Pneumonia
PY  - 2023
AU  - Alipanah-Lechner, N
AU  - Hurst-Hopf, J
AU  - Delucchi, K
AU  - Brown Swigart, L
AU  - Willmore, A
AU  - Matthay, MA
AU  - Liu, K
AU  - Esserman, L
AU  - Calfee, CS
AB  - RATIONALE: Latent class analysis (LCA) of clinical and cytokine data has identified two phenotypes of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) with different clinical outcomes and treatment responses. In COVID‐19‐related ARDS, LCA of patient data from a single center early in the pandemic revealed significant overlap with established ARDS phenotypes, but data on biomarkers of lung injury and inflammation were unavailable1. We studied biological heterogeneity and clinical outcomes in a multi‐institutional platform randomized controlled trial of patients with severe COVID‐19 pneumonia (I‐SPY COVID)2. METHODS: Data from the first 400 participants enrolled from October 2020 until October 2021 were analyzed. Eligible participants were hospitalized adults with confirmed SARS‐CoV‐2 infection, regardless of randomization arm, who required >6 L of supplemental oxygen and had plasma collected at trial enrollment. Seventeen hypothesis‐directed protein biomarkers were measured at enrollment using multiplex Luminex panels or single analyte enzyme linked immunoassay methods (ELISA). LCA of protein biomarkers and baseline clinical variables was used to search for latent phenotypes. The association of phenotypes with 28‐day and 60‐day mortality was assessed using Pearson's Chi‐squared test. A validated parsimonious model using interleukin‐8, bicarbonate, and protein C was used for comparison with ARDS phenotypes3. RESULTS: Average participant age was 60 + 14 years; 67% were male, and 60‐day mortality was 29%. At trial enrollment, 26% of participants required positive pressure ventilation and 97% were receiving corticosteroid treatment. Using standard LCA methods, a two‐class model was deemed the optimal fit, with 311 (78%) participants in class 1 and 89 (22%) in class 2. Class 2 participants were older, more likely to be on mechanical ventilation, and had higher creatinine, tumor necrosis factor receptor‐1 (TNFr1), neutrophil/lymphocyte ratio, and brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) levels (Figure). Time from symptom onset was similar between phenotypes. Class 2 participants had significantly higher 28‐day (43% vs 20%, p <0.01) and 60‐day mortality (47% vs 24%, p<0.01). One participant was classified as hyper inflammatory using the parsimonious ARDS model, and this participant had a Class 2 phenotype. CONCLUSIONS: We identified two novel phenotypes of severe COVID‐19 pneumonia with minimal overlap with previously described ARDS phenotypes. Class 2 phenotype was characterized by elevated creatinine, TNFr1, neutrophil/lymphocyte ratio, and BNP as well as increased 28‐day and 60‐day mortality. Lack of overlap with prior ARDS phenotypes may be due to differences in host‐response and/or treatment between COVID‐19 and classical ARDS, as well as inclusion of patients with milder hypoxemic respiratory failure without ARDS.
