AN  - f07610c613ed7026a3c868623a1d9527f297cde4
TI  - Impaired Neutralising Antibodies to SARS-CoV-2 Omicron BA.2 Variant and Preserved Cellular Immunogenicity Following Heterologous Prime-Boost Vaccination with an Inactivated Vaccine Followed by ChAdOx1-nCoV-19 in Patients with Systemic Autoimmune Rheumatic Diseases
PY  - 2022
AU  - Porntip Intapiboon
AU  - Parichat Uae-areewongsa
AU  - Jomkwan Ongarj
AU  - Ratchanon Sophonmanee
AU  - Purilap Seepathomnarong
AU  - Bunya Seeyankem
AU  - Smonrapat Surasombatpattana
AU  - Nawamin Pinpathomrat
AB  - Background: Neutralisation of the SARS-CoV-2-Omicron BA·2 variant in patients with systemic autoimmune rheumatic diseases (SARDs) has not been reported, and information on the cellular response is inconclusive. Heterologous prime-boost vaccination is a promising strategy for improving immunogenicity. We report vaccine immunogenicity with an inactivated vaccine followed by an adenoviral vector vaccine in patients with SARDs. Methods: This prospective study investigated vaccine immunogenicity with CoronaVac followed by ChAdOx1 nCoV-19 in patients with SARDs  vs·  age- and sex-matched healthy controls. Anti-RBD antibodies, neutralising antibodies to Omicron BA·2 [plaque reduction neutralisation test (PRNT)], and T-cell responses were measured 4 weeks after vaccination. Seropositivity was defined as IgG ≥7·15 binding antibody units (BAU)/mL. PRNT was determined by 50% inhibition of viral infectivity. T-cell phenotypes and effector cytokine production were analysed using flow cytometry. Adverse events were evaluated by telephone interviews. Findings: Median (IQR) anti-RBD-IgG levels were lower in patients with SARDs than in healthy controls (p=0·003). Neutralising antibodies against the Omicron BA·2 variant followed the same trend (p=0·004). T-cell analysis revealed higher levels of S1-specific IFN-γ- and TNF-α-secreting CD4+ T cells (p<0·001, p=0·0322, respectively) in patients with SARDs than in healthy controls. Effector cytokine production by CD8+ T cells was consistent with Th responses. Glucocorticoids, mycophenolate mofetil, and azathioprine were associated with humoral impairment. Adverse events did not differ between groups. Interpretation:This vaccine regimen revealed mild impaired humoral, especially neutralisation to the SARS-CoV-2 Omicron BA.2 variant, preserving cellular immunogenicity. This regimen may be an alternative for individuals for whom mRNA vaccines are contraindicated. Funding Information: This project received funding from the Faculty of Medicine, Prince of Songkla University, and the National Vaccine Institute. Declaration of Interests: The authors declare no competing financial or non-financial interests. Ethics Approval Statement: The study was conducted according to the Declaration of Helsinki and Good Clinical Practice guidelines. Approval was obtained from the Human Research Ethics Committee of the Faculty of Medicine, Prince of Songkla University (REC·64– 421–14–1, Date of Approval 7 October 2021). All participants provided written informed consent.
UR  - https://www.epistemonikos.org/en/documents/f07610c613ed7026a3c868623a1d9527f297cde4
T2  - SSRN
DO  - 10.2139/ssrn.4133614
