TY  - JOUR
AN  - f6ac11a4ce828cc058c4ef07560fa0f3e5b8093d
TI  - Real-World Effectiveness and Safety of Nirmatrelvir-Ritonavir (Paxlovid)-Treated for COVID-19 Patients with Onset of More than Five Days: A Retrospective Cohort Study
PY  - 2024
AU  - Ye Qiu
AU  - Hao Wen
AU  - Haoru Wang
AU  - Wenjun Sun
AU  - Guangchao Li
AU  - Shao-Qiang Li
AU  - Yan Wang
AU  - Jingnan Zhai
AU  - Yang-Qing Zhan
AU  - Yutian Su
AU  - Zhi-Wei Long
AU  - Zhengtu Li
AU  - Feng Ye
AB  - Background: Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir (Paxlovid) has received emergency use authorization from the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) owing to its effectiveness and safety. However, data on the effectiveness and safety of Paxlovid use in coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) patients with onset of more than five days are lacking. Methods: A real-world retrospective study was performed during the outbreak involving the SARS-CoV-2 BA.5.2 subvariant. Hospitalized COVID-19 patients (including mild, moderate, severe and critical cases) were divided into three groups: Paxlovid treatment within (Group A) or more than (Group B) five days of COVID-19 onset and no Paxlovid treatment during more than five days of COVID-19 onset with only basic symptomatic treatment (Group C). Group B was further divided into Group B1 (treated at the time of COVID-19 onset at 6-10 days) and Group B2 (treated after COVID-19 onset of more than 10 days) based on Paxlovid treatment time. Endpoints were all-cause 28-day mortality, improvement in clinical classification, and a composite endpoint of disease progression, viral load and virus elimination time. Safety was assessed by comparing adverse events reported during treatment in each group. Findings: During the period, 248 hospitalized COVID-19 patients, including 55 in Group A, 170 in Group B, and 23 in Group C, were enrolled. There were no significant differences in the clinical classification improvement rate [80.0% (16/20) vs. 81.3% (52/64), p=1.000; 60.0% (21/35) vs. 55.7% (59/106), p=0.653, respectively] or all-cause 28-day mortality [0% (0/20) vs. 1.6% (1/64), p=1.000; 11.4% (4/35) vs. 6.6% (7/106), p=0.576, respectively] between Groups A and B for nonsevere and severe cases. However, the clinical classification improvement rate in Group B was markedly higher than that in Group C [81.3% (52/64) vs. 50.0% (6/12), p=0.049] among nonsevere cases. Furthermore, the clinical classification improvement rate in Group B1 was markedly higher than that among Group B2 [66.0% (33/50) vs. 46.4% (26/56), p=0.043] among severe cases. Cycle threshold values of the N and ORF genes in Group B were significantly increased after Paxlovid treatment [31.14 (IQR 26.81-33.93) vs. 38.14 (IQR 36.92-40.00), p<0.001; 31.33 (IQR 26.00-33.47) vs. 38.62 (IQR 35.62-40.00), p<0.001, respectively]. In addition, the virus elimination time in Group A and Group B1 was significantly shorter than that in Group B2 [19.0 (IQR 10.3-21.8) vs. 23.5 (IQR 19.0-29.0) days, p=0.045; 16.5 (IQR 9.8-22.3) vs. 23.5 (IQR 19.0-29.0) days, p=0.001, respectively]. No significant differences in reported adverse events of neurological disease (p=0.571), liver injury (p=0.960) or kidney injury (p=0.193) between Group A and Group B were found. Interpretation: Paxlovid treatment within ten days of onset can shorten the disease course of COVID-19 by reducing the viral load. Paxlovid is effective and safe in treating COVID-19 with onset of more than five or even ten days when patients have a high viral load. Funding: This research was supported by the National Key R&D Program of China (No. 2023YFC3041700) and R&D Program of Guangzhou National Laboratory (No. SRPG23-001). Declaration of Interest: The authors declare that the research was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationship. The authors declare that they have no conflicts of interest. Ethical Approval: The Institutional Ethics Review Board of the First Affiliated Hospital of Guangzhou Medical University approved the study (No. 2020-92), in accordance with Good Clinical Practice and the Declaration of Helsinki. Written informed consent was waived due to the urgent need to collect data during the upsurge of cases in the Omicron outbreak.
